/-!
# Equation 52.7.0.1 at a sheaf of ideals of finite type

Section 52.7 writes the display [Stacks, Tag 0A0I]

```
Rf_*(ℱ^∧) = (Rf_*ℱ)^∧
```

for a morphism of schemes `f : X ⟶ S`, a **quasi-coherent sheaf of ideals of finite type**
`ℐ ⊆ 𝒪_S` and a quasi-coherent `ℱ` on `X`, the completion on `X` being taken along
`f^{-1}ℐ · 𝒪_X` and the one on `S` along `ℐ`. `PushforwardCommutesCompletionSpaces.lean`,
`PushforwardCommutesCompletionGenerated.lean` and
`PushforwardCommutesCompletionConstructedIdeal.lean` read the display at a morphism of ringed
spaces and at a morphism of schemes for two sheaves of ideals *generated by finitely many global
sections*, which is strictly more data than the source asks for: a sheaf of ideals of finite
type is only **locally** generated by finitely many sections.

This file removes that restriction. The only use the source makes of the hypotheses on `ℐ` is
to know that the derived completion functors exist, i.e. [Stacks, Tag 099F], and that tag is
available at a sheaf of ideals of finite type: it is the instance
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.isRightAdjoint_ι_isDerivedComplete` of `FiniteTypeReflector.lean`,
proved from [Stacks, Tag 099E] (`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.exists_isGlobalExtendedCech` of
`FiniteTypeExtendedCech.lean`), whose object `K ⟶ 𝒪` is built from *all* the finite families of
local generators at once, so that the locality of the finite-type hypothesis is what the
construction consumes. Feeding it to the ringed-site statements of
`PushforwardCommutesCompletion.lean`, which ask for exactly that conclusion and nothing about
how it was reached, gives the display at the source's own data.

Quasi-coherence of `ℐ` and of `ℱ` is not needed: in the source those enter only through
[Stacks, Tag 099F], and on a scheme a quasi-coherent sheaf of ideals of finite type is of finite
type in the sense of `SheafOfModules.IsFiniteType`.

## Main results

* `Stacks.RingedSpace.isDerivedComplete_derivedPushforward_derivedCompletion_of_isFiniteType`
  and `Scheme.isDerivedComplete_derivedPushforward_derivedCompletion_of_isFiniteType`:
  the left hand side of the display is derived complete along `ℐ`, for an **arbitrary** object
  of `D(𝒪_X)`, at a morphism of ringed spaces and at a morphism of schemes, for sheaves of
  ideals of finite type.
* `RingedSpace.isIso_derivedCompletionPushforwardComparison_of_isFiniteType_of_isDerivedComplete`
  and `Scheme.isIso_derivedCompletionPushforwardComparison_of_isFiniteType_of_isDerivedComplete`:
  the display itself, `(Rf_*ℱ)^∧ ⟶ Rf_*(ℱ^∧)` invertible, at sheaves of ideals of finite type
  and for an object `ℱ` derived complete along `ℐ'`.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.isDerivedComplete_derivedPushforward_derivedCompletion_idealSheafData`
  and `Scheme.isIso_derivedCompletionPushforwardComparison_idealSheafData_of_isDerivedComplete`:
  the same two statements written in the source's own words — at **quasi-coherent** sheaves of
  ideals of finite type, that is at `AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.IdealSheafData`s read on the small
  Zariski ringed site by `Stacks.Algebraization.idealSheafSiteSubmodule`. On a locally
  Noetherian scheme the finite-type hypothesis is automatic, by the instance
  `Stacks.Algebraization.isFiniteType_idealSheafSiteSubmodule`.
* `Scheme.isIso_derivedCompletionPushforwardComparison_idealSheafData_inverseImageIdealSheaf`:
  the display for an **arbitrary** object of `D(𝒪_X)` — in particular every complex of
  quasi-coherent modules, which is the analogue [Stacks, Tag 0AKL] attributes to
  [HL-P, Theorem 6.5] — at a morphism of schemes, a quasi-coherent sheaf of ideals `ℐ` of finite
  type and the sheaf of ideals `ℐ' = f^{-1}ℐ · 𝒪_X` built out of it. It is
  `RingedSite.isIso_derivedCompletionPushforwardComparison_inverseImageIdealSheaf_of_isFiniteType`
  at the morphism of ringed sites underlying `f`.

## Further results

* `Stacks.RingedSpace.isRightAdjoint_ι_isDerivedComplete_of_isFiniteType`:
  **[Stacks, Tag 099F]** read on the small Zariski site of a ringed space. Instance search does
  not find `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.isRightAdjoint_ι_isDerivedComplete` at a ringed site of
  this shape, exactly as `PushforwardCompletionTopos.lean` records for the site presenting a
  ringed topos, so it is restated here.
* `Stacks.RingedSpace.isFiniteType_inverseImageIdealSheaf_of_isFiniteType`: at a morphism between
  the small Zariski sites of two ringed spaces, `f^{-1}ℐ · 𝒪_X` is of finite type when `ℐ` is —
  `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.isFiniteType_inverseImageIdealSheaf`, restated for the same
  reason.
* `Stacks.RingedSpace.isFiniteType_generatedIdealSheaf`: at a ringed space the sheaf of ideals
  generated by finitely many global sections of `𝒪_X` is of finite type. This is what makes the
  statements here contain the globally generated ones of
  `PushforwardCommutesCompletionGenerated.lean`: the trivial covering of the terminal object by
  every open set turns global generators into local generators, which is
  `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.isFiniteType_generatedIdealSheaf` together with
  `HasBinaryProducts (TopologicalSpace.Opens X)`.

## What is and is not covered of the display

At an independent parameter `ℐ'` the restriction is on the **object**, not on the data: those
statements either restrict `ℱ` to the objects derived complete along `ℐ'` — where
`Rf_*(ℱ^∧) = Rf_*ℱ = (Rf_*ℱ)^∧` — or assert derived completeness of `Rf_*(ℱ^∧)` for an
arbitrary `ℱ`. At the source's own `ℐ' = f^{-1}ℐ · 𝒪_X` there is no restriction:
`Scheme.isIso_derivedCompletionPushforwardComparison_idealSheafData_inverseImageIdealSheaf`
is the display for every `ℱ`.

The invertibility of the comparison for an arbitrary `ℱ` is the assertion of
[Stacks, Tag 0A0G], and it is proved at the same data — a sheaf of ideals of finite type — by
`RingedSite.isIso_derivedCompletionPushforwardComparison_inverseImageIdealSheaf_of_isFiniteType`
of `PushforwardCommutesCompletionLocalToGlobal.lean`, for `ℐ'` the ideal sheaf
`f^{-1}ℐ · 𝒪_X` that Section 52.7 constructs. The source's own route to it — rewrite both sides
by the Koszul formula of [Stacks, Tag 0A0E] and finish with the invertibility of the projection
formula [Stacks, Tag 0944], of which this library has only the map,
`Stacks.Cohomology.ProjectionFormulaOnRingedTopoi.projectionFormulaMap` of [Stacks, Tag 0B56] —
is *not* the route taken, and
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.isIso_derivedCompletionPushforwardComparison_of_isIso_map_unit`,
the criterion that computation would be fed into, is not what closed it: the globally generated
case is reached through the Čech formula of [Stacks, Tag 099B] and the general case from it by
localization on the target site.

The second sheaf of ideals is a parameter here, asked to be of finite type and to receive
`f^♯(f^{-1}ℐ)`. The source's own `ℐ' = f^{-1}ℐ · 𝒪_X` is such a sheaf of ideals — it receives
`f^♯(f^{-1}ℐ)` by construction, and it is of finite type because `ℐ` is, being locally generated
by the images of local generators of `ℐ`. Where this library builds that ideal sheaf, namely out
of global generators of `ℐ` in `PushforwardCommutesCompletionConstructedIdeal.lean`, both facts
are proved: `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.pushforwardIdealCompatible_pushGlobalSections` and
`Stacks.RingedSpace.isFiniteType_generatedIdealSheaf`. So the two sheaves of ideals are kept as
independent parameters linked by
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.PushforwardIdealCompatible`, as they are throughout this
directory.

*Where `f^{-1}ℐ · 𝒪_X` lives for a quasi-coherent `ℐ`, and what it would take to use it.* For a
morphism of schemes it is `AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.IdealSheafData.comap I f` of
`Mathlib/AlgebraicGeometry/IdealSheaf/Functorial.lean`, the kernel of
`pullback.fst f I.subschemeι` — the ideal of the scheme-theoretic preimage, which is the image
of `f^*ℐ ⟶ 𝒪_X`. Substituting it for the parameter `ℐ'` in the quasi-coherent statements below
needs two bridges this library does not have: that the site-level `f^♯` of
`Stacks.RingedSpace.toRingedSiteHom` agrees with `f.app`, and that membership in
`Stacks.Algebraization.idealSheafSiteSubmodule` over an arbitrary open may be checked on an
affine cover. Given those, `AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.IdealSheafData.le_map_comap`
(`ℐ ≤ (ℐ.comap f).map f`) together with `AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.IdealSheafData.ideal_map` —
which carries `[QuasiCompact f]` and asks the preimage of the affine open to be affine — reads
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.PushforwardIdealCompatible` on such an open. Finite-typeness of
the comap would be a third bridge.

